Antiphospholipid Syndrome (Mild Obstetric Phenotype)
Autoimmune Conditions Mild Severity
No history of systemic venous/arterial thrombosis, managed preventatively during pregnancy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-002
Reason for Visit: Acute unilateral calf cramping in a high-risk pregnant patient requiring DVT exclusion.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and partner
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate acute lower limb pain in an early-pregnancy patient with a known prothrombotic autoimmune condition. Ruling out a deep vein thrombosis was strictly necessary to prevent potential pulmonary embolism and maternal/fetal morbidity prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Sudden pain in my right calf and I am terrified of getting a blood clot.
History of Present Illness: Patient is 9 weeks pregnant and reports sudden onset of right calf cramping starting two hours ago. The symptom evolved from a dull ache to a sharp cramp when walking. Patient has a known positive aPL antibodies with single early miscarriage history. Currently experiencing severe anxiety regarding potential thrombosis.
Functional Status: Independent, currently resting in bed
Social/Safety Concerns: High anxiety regarding pregnancy viability.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/75 | HR: 98 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused, noticeably anxious.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular and peripheral vascular exam reveals no gross asymmetry in lower limbs. Right calf is soft, non-tender to deep palpation. Homan's sign negative. No erythema or palpable cords. Review of systems confirms no history of systemic venous/arterial thrombosis, managed preventatively during pregnancy.
Environmental Observations: Safe residential layout, ground floor access, temperature appropriate.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Taking low-dose aspirin as prescribed.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Musculoskeletal Calf Cramp in the setting of Antiphospholipid Syndrome (Mild Obstetric Phenotype) - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Soft, non-tender calf, negative Homan's sign, and absence of unilateral edema or erythema.

Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Afebrile status, no skin erythema, warmth, or induration.

Achilles Tendinopathy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of localized tenderness over the Achilles insertion and pain-free active plantarflexion.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute unilateral lower limb pain superimposed on a highly prothrombotic autoimmune baseline (aPL positive, pregnant). Triage correctly flagged the absolute necessity of ruling out an acute deep vein thrombosis. Clinical evaluation demonstrated no objective evidence of DVT, confirming musculoskeletal cramping likely secondary to normal pregnancy physiology. Immediate clinical assessment was imperative to avoid potentially fatal pulmonary embolism and to manage severe maternal distress. This evaluation could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period given the rapidly actionable nature of thromboembolic events.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance and gentle calf stretching exercises demonstrated.
Positive Behavioral Change: Drink one large glass of water immediately upon waking and perform gentle calf stretches twice daily. Staying hydrated and maintaining muscle flexibility will proactively reduce the frequency of benign cramping, rewarding you with uninterrupted sleep.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine obstetrician or GP review in 1-2 weeks.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If you experience sudden shortness of breath, chest pain, coughing up blood, or if the calf becomes visibly swollen, red, and hot to the touch, you must call 000 immediately or present to the nearest Emergency Department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Musculoskeletal cramps resolve quickly with stretching and hydration. Prothrombotic risk remains unchanged and requires strict adherence to preventative medications.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency care if the pain worsens significantly, the leg swells rapidly, or any respiratory symptoms develop.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The pain is a benign muscle cramp, common in early pregnancy, rather than a blood clot. Your preventative medication remains appropriate.
Actionable Steps: Complete your stretching routine every morning and evening to actively prevent recurrence.

